%I A402193 #13 Nov 27 2026 13:46:56
%S A402193 7,11,84919,84947,85243,85247,85259,85363,85381,93103,93113,93131,
%T A402193 93169,93179,93199,93241,93251,93481,93493,93497,93503,93529,93811,
%U A402193 93827,93851,94837,94841,94847,96661,96697,96739,96749,96823,96827,96907,96911,179527,179689,179693
%N A402193 Primes p for which pi_{24;13,19}(p) - pi_{24;1,7}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A402193 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,7} and S = {13,19}, corresponding to T = {chi_8, chi_{-24}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A402193 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_8(q) + chi_{-24}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A402193 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;13,19}(x) > pi_{24;1,7}(x) has logarithmic density 0.97869, making the race between {1,7} and {13,19} the 18th most biased among the 21 races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A402193 Let D(x) = pi_{24;13,19}(x) - pi_{24;1,7}(x). Below x0 = 8*10^12 there are respectively 34876145777, 34876274835, 34876248066, 34876268167, 34876282644, 34876258940, 34876298151, and 34876294229 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 186952. There are 10 sign-changing zones giving a total of 320947 terms below x0:
%C A402193   (1) a(1..2) = 7-11;
%C A402193   (2) a(3..9) = 84919-85381 (there are 42 primes a(3) <= p <= a(9) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -4 at p = 85009);
%C A402193   (3) a(10..36) = 93103-96911 (there are 301 primes a(10) <= p <= a(36) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -11 at p = 94447);
%C A402193   (4) a(37..91) = 179527-185917 (there are 274 primes a(37) <= p <= a(91) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -7 at p = 185167);
%C A402193   (5) a(92..125) = 66703753-66783179 (there are 96 primes a(92) <= p <= a(125) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -4 at p = 66776791);
%C A402193   (6) a(126..8083) = 1196926183-1271718143 (there are 1699576 primes a(126) <= p <= a(8083) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -319 at p = 1247213329);
%C A402193   (7) a(8084..9800) = 1879278607-1906485827 (there are 1222820 primes a(8084) <= p <= a(9800) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -398 at p = 1881396007);
%C A402193   (8) a(9801..11351) = 23336838361-23344643291 (there are 272765 primes a(9801) <= p <= a(11351) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -248 at p = 23340021247);
%C A402193   (9) a(11352..233177) = 971773930711-1105242387959 (there are 4091772490 primes a(11352) <= p <= a(233177) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -17011 at p = 1017930708943);
%C A402193   (10) a(233178..320947) = 1369175812489-1390136982463 (there are 58452545 primes a(233178) <= p <= a(320947) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2614 at p = 1389498012751).
%C A402193 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 4153420911.
%H A402193 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/b402193.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..11351</a> (the first 8 sign-changing zones)
%H A402193 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402193 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193_1.txt">Sign-changing zones of five prime number races modulo 24</a>.
%H A402193 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402193 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..320947</a> (all terms below 8*10^12).
%H A402193 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A402193 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A402193 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A402193 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A402193 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==7, k--); if(p%24==13||p%24==19, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A402193 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A402193 nonn,new
%O A402193 1,1
%A A402193 _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_, Nov 15 2026
